const e=[{id:"pe-l-company-person",moduleId:"m09-company",title:"The company that owes the money is not you",minutes:20,summary:"Two friends open a shop. The day they register a company, the law adds a third person to the story, and that third person owes the debts.",plants:["pe-c-co-01","pe-c-co-02","pe-c-co-03","pe-c-co-04"],prepares:["pe-p-company-01"],reading:[{bookId:"companies-act-annotation",where:"the opening provisions on incorporation and the effect of registration"}],source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777); Salomon v A Salomon and Co Ltd.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"The Act is free at lom.agc.gov.my. This lesson deliberately cites no section numbers except by describing the rule; before you rely on any of it, read the incorporation provisions of the Companies Act 2016 and check for amendments.",plate:"debt-wall",plateCaption:"The debt stops at the company. That wall is the whole of why anybody incorporates, and the reason a member cannot usually be pursued.",readMinutes:6,sections:[{id:"sec-pe-l-company-person-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Two people sell nasi lemak on credit. If the shop fails, who owes the rice supplier?",h:"Why anyone bothers to register",body:[{t:"p",text:"Aina and Rosli sell nasi lemak from a rented shoplot in Klang. They buy their rice on credit. If the shop fails, who owes the rice supplier?"},{t:"p",text:"If they registered nothing, the answer is both of them, personally. Their savings. Their motorbike. The money in their bank accounts. Nothing stands between the debt and them."},{t:"p",text:"Registering a company changes that answer. It is the main reason small businesses do it."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-company-person-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Registration creates a body corporate with a legal personality separate from its owners.",h:"What registration creates",body:[{t:"p",text:"They pay a fee and file forms with the Companies Commission of Malaysia, known as SSM. A few days later, Nasi Lemak Aina Sdn Bhd exists."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Two years later the shop fails, owing the rice supplier RM9,000. There is nothing left in the business, and the supplier wants paying. Whose RM9,000 is it?",options:["Aina's and Rosli's, because they ordered the rice","Aina's and Rosli's, but only half each","The company's, and the supplier must look to the company"],answer:2,reveal:"The company's. The supplier's argument is now with a thing rather than with two people, and that thing has no savings and no motorbike. The first answer is the one nearly everybody gives, and it would have been exactly right the day before they registered. One filing changed it. That is worth sitting with, because it is the whole reason small businesses bother."},{t:"p",text:"It is not just a name on a signboard. In the eyes of the law it is a new person, separate from Aina and separate from Rosli."},{t:"rule",text:"A company registered under the Companies Act 2016 is a body corporate. It has a legal personality separate from the people who own it.",source:"Companies Act 2016"},{t:"p",text:"That person can own things. It can sign a lease. It can sue, and be sued, under its own name."},{t:"example",text:"The rice supplier's account is now with the company. If the bill goes unpaid, the supplier sues the company. Not Aina. Not Rosli."},{t:"p",text:"Lawyers call this **separate legal personality**. The phrase sounds abstract. The idea is the most practical one in this module."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-company-person-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Salomon: the company was a real person in law even though one man held almost every share.",h:"The famous case, in one paragraph",body:[{t:"p",text:"A man named Salomon ran a boot business and turned it into a company. He held almost every share. When the business failed, the creditors argued that the company was really just him in a hat."},{t:"p",text:"The court disagreed. The company had been properly registered, so it was a separate person, and its debts were its own. The case is *Salomon v A Salomon and Co Ltd*."},{t:"caution",text:"It is an English decision from 1897, and Malaysian courts apply the principle. Do not read the age of a case as a sign that it is stale. Some old decisions hold up the whole building."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-company-person-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Limited liability limits what a member risks, not what the company owes.",h:"What limited liability limits",body:[{t:"p",text:"Aina pays RM500 for her shares. Rosli pays RM500 for his. That money now belongs to the company."},{t:"p",text:"Say the company collapses owing RM80,000. Aina and Rosli lose their RM500 each. They do not have to find the RM80,000."},{t:"rule",text:"A member of a company limited by shares is not liable for the company's debts merely because they are a member. Their exposure is limited to any amount still unpaid on their shares.",source:"Companies Act 2016"},{t:"p",text:"That is what *limited* means in Sendirian Berhad. It limits the owner's loss. It does not limit the company's."},{t:"caution",text:"The company still owes every ringgit. Creditors take everything the company owns first, down to the last chair. Only then do they run out of things to take."},{t:"compare",title:"What *limited* limits, and what it does not",left:{h:"Aina's loss is limited",items:["She paid RM500 for her shares.","If the company collapses she loses that RM500.","She does not have to find the RM80,000.","Her savings and her motorbike are hers."]},right:{h:"The company's debt is not",items:["The company still owes every ringgit of it.","Creditors take everything the company owns first.","Down to the last chair in the shop.","Only then do they run out of things to take."]}}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-company-person-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The commonest hole in the protection is a personal guarantee the bank asks for anyway.",h:"The commonest hole in the protection",body:[{t:"p",text:"A bank is asked to lend the shop RM30,000. The bank looks at a company with two years of history and RM1,000 of paid-up capital. It is not reassured."},{t:"p",text:"So it asks Aina to sign a **personal guarantee**. That is a separate promise: if the company does not pay, she will."},{t:"example",text:"The shop closes owing the bank RM28,000. The bank sues the company for the debt and Aina on her guarantee. Rosli signed nothing, so Rosli owes nothing."},{t:"caution",text:"Notice what did the damage. Not her shares, and not her seat on the board. A contract she signed with her own hand. Small business owners sign these routinely and are shocked later."},{t:"p",text:"Guarantees are not the only way through. A statute can put liability on a director, and a court can sometimes look behind a company that was set up to dodge something. The next lesson deals with both."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"The question a lender's paperwork turns on",caption:"Notice what did the damage. Not her shares, and not her seat on the board. A contract she signed with her own hand.",alt:"A question — did she sign a personal guarantee? — branching two ways. No: the company owes the debt, and her exposure stops at any amount still unpaid on her shares. Yes: she owes it as well, under a separate promise made in her own name.",question:"Did she sign a personal guarantee?",branches:[{cond:"no",label:"The company owes it",note:"her exposure stops at her shares"},{cond:"yes",label:"She owes it too",note:"a separate promise, in her own name"}]}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-company-person-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"There are three parties in this story, not two: Aina, Rosli, and the company.",h:"What to hold on to",body:[{t:"p",text:"One idea carries the rest of the module. There are three parties in this story, not two: Aina, Rosli, and the company."},{t:"p",text:"When a company question confuses you, ask two things. Who did the law treat as acting? Who owes the money? Most of the time the answer to both is the company."}]}],quiz:[{id:"pe-q-cop-1",q:"Aina and Rosli register a company. The company then buys rice on credit and cannot pay. Who owes the supplier?",options:["Aina and Rosli, because they run the shop","The company","Whoever placed the order","Aina, Rosli and the company together"],answer:1,why:"The company made the contract, so the company owes the money. The trap is thinking that the person who did the shopping is the person who owes.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777); the company contracts in its own name"},{id:"pe-q-cop-2",q:"What does the word 'limited' limit?",options:["How much the company can owe","How long the company can trade","How much the owners can lose","How many shares can be issued"],answer:2,why:"The company still owes the full debt. Only the members' exposure is capped, at whatever is still unpaid on their shares.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777); members' liability is limited to any amount unpaid on their shares"},{id:"pe-q-cop-3",q:"Salomon owned almost all the shares and ran the whole business himself. What did the court hold?",options:["The company was still a separate person, and the debts were its own","One-man control made the company a sham","He was liable because he was also a creditor","The company was separate only for tax"],answer:0,why:"Total control is not a reason to ignore the company. That is the exact point the case settled, and it is why control alone will not lift the veil.",source:"Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd [1897] AC 22"},{id:"pe-q-cop-4",q:"The bank can reach Aina's savings but not Rosli's. Why?",options:["She signed a personal guarantee and he did not","She holds more shares than he does","She is a director and he is not","She was the one who dealt with the bank"],answer:0,why:"The guarantee is a separate contract she made herself. Being a shareholder or a director would not have exposed her savings.",source:"Standard contract principle; a personal guarantee is a separate contract"},{id:"pe-q-cop-5",q:"The company owns the shop's oven. Who else owns it?",options:["Aina and Rosli, half each","The bank, until the loan is repaid","Aina and Rosli jointly with the company","Nobody. The members own shares, not the company's things"],answer:3,why:"Owning shares is not owning the company's property. Missing this is what makes separate personality feel like a technicality when it is not.",source:"Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd [1897] AC 22; members own shares, not the company's property"}]},{id:"pe-l-company-roles",moduleId:"m09-company",title:"Who runs a company, who owns it, and when a court looks behind it",minutes:22,summary:"In a small company the same two people own it and run it. The law still keeps those jobs apart, and gives each one different powers and different duties.",plants:["pe-c-co-05","pe-c-co-06","pe-c-co-07","pe-c-co-08"],prepares:["pe-p-company-01"],reading:[{bookId:"companies-act-annotation",where:"the notes to ss 213, 214, 218 and 346"}],source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), ss 213, 214, 218 and 346; Salomon v A Salomon and Co Ltd.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read ss 213, 214, 218 and 346 in the current text at lom.agc.gov.my before relying on any of this. They are short. The 2016 Act replaced the Companies Act 1965, so anything written before 2017 may describe repealed law.",plate:"boardroom",plateCaption:"Directors run it, members own it, and the meeting is where the two meet. Most company disputes are about which hat someone was wearing.",readMinutes:8,sections:[{id:"sec-pe-l-company-roles-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Two people wearing two hats each — owner and director are different jobs.",h:"Two people, three jobs",body:[{t:"p",text:"Aina and Rosli own all the shares in their nasi lemak company. They also run it day to day. On paper they are wearing two hats each."},{t:"list",items:["The **company** owns the business, makes the contracts and owes the debts.","The **members**, also called shareholders, own the shares. They own the company, not the things the company owns.","The **directors** manage it. They make the decisions and sign for it."]},{t:"p",text:"In a small Sdn Bhd the same two people hold every hat. In a large one the owners and the managers are strangers."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not skip this because it looks obvious. Most errors in this module come from treating a shareholder's complaint as the company's, or the company's as a shareholder's."},{t:"table",caption:"In a small Sdn Bhd the same two people hold every hat, which is exactly why the hats have to be kept apart. Most errors in this module come from mixing them up.",columns:[{label:"Who"},{label:"What they own, or do"},{label:"The error to avoid"}],rows:[["The company","Owns the business, makes the contracts, owes the debts","Treating its loss as a shareholder's loss"],["The members","Own the shares — not the things the company owns","Thinking a shareholder may direct the managers"],["The directors","Manage it. They decide, and they sign for it","Treating the company's money as their own"]]}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-company-roles-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The till money belongs to the company, and taking it is not a loan to yourself.",h:"The company's money is not their money",body:[{t:"p",text:"The till takes RM400 on a good day. Aina cannot put it in her handbag."},{t:"p",text:"That cash belongs to the company. She can be paid a salary as an employee, fees as a director, or a dividend as a shareholder. Each of the three has its own rules."},{t:"example",text:"Rosli pays his son's school fees from the company account and writes nothing down. That is not a private matter between two friends. It is company money taken by a director."},{t:"caution",text:"This is the commonest trouble in small Malaysian companies. It turns a quarrel between partners into a claim the company itself can bring."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-company-roles-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A director acts for a proper purpose, in good faith, in the company's best interest.",h:"What a director must do",body:[{t:"p",text:"A director is trusted with someone else's business. The Act spells out what that trust requires."},{t:"rule",text:"A director shall at all times exercise his powers for a proper purpose and in good faith in the best interest of the company.",source:"Companies Act 2016, s 213(1)"},{t:"rule",text:"A director shall exercise reasonable care, skill and diligence with the knowledge, skill and experience reasonably expected of a director with the same responsibilities, plus any extra knowledge or experience he actually has.",source:"Companies Act 2016, s 213(2)"},{t:"p",text:"Read the second one twice. There is a floor that every director must meet. If you happen to be an accountant, your own floor is higher."},{t:"rule",text:"A director shall not improperly use the company's property, position or information to gain a benefit or to cause harm to the company.",source:"Companies Act 2016, s 218"},{t:"example",text:"A customer offers the company a catering job. Aina takes it in her own name and keeps the fee. The objection is not that the company lost money. It is that the chance belonged to the company."},{t:"caution",text:"A bad decision is treated differently from a disloyal one. Section 214 shelters a director who made a business judgement in good faith and without a personal interest. It must also be a decision made on a reasonably informed basis, which he rationally believed was in the company's interest."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-company-roles-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Shareholders do not manage, and cannot hand the directors instructions on a Tuesday.",h:"What a shareholder can actually do",body:[{t:"p",text:"People overestimate this badly. A shareholder does not manage the company and cannot hand the directors instructions on a Tuesday afternoon."},{t:"list",items:["Vote at meetings, in proportion to the shares held.","Appoint directors, and in the right circumstances remove them.","Receive a dividend, but only if the company declares one and is permitted to pay it.","See the records and accounts the Act requires the company to keep.","Sell the shares, if a buyer exists and the company's constitution allows it.","Ask a court for help, where the company is being run oppressively."]},{t:"caution",text:"In a small private company the last two are weak. Nobody wants to buy a minority stake in a family business, so an unhappy holder is stuck. That is exactly why the court remedy matters here."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-company-roles-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"When something goes wrong, ask who the injured party is before asking who is angry.",h:"When something goes wrong, who sues?",body:[{t:"p",text:"Rosli finds out that Aina has been taking catering jobs privately. Who is the injured party?"},{t:"p",text:"The company. The duty in s 213 is owed to the company, so the company is the right claimant."},{t:"p",text:"But directors decide whether the company sues, and Aina is a director. That is the whole difficulty, and the Act answers it with two routes."},{t:"list",items:["A **derivative action**. A member asks the court for permission to sue in the company's name, because the company will not sue itself.","An **oppression** claim under s 346. A member complains that the company's affairs are run in a way that is oppressive to them, or unfairly disregards their interests."]},{t:"caution",text:"Keep the two apart. A derivative action enforces the company's right. An oppression claim enforces the member's own right. Picking the wrong one is a standard way to lose a good case."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-company-roles-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Lifting the veil happens far less often than people expect, and control alone is never enough.",h:"When a court looks behind the company",body:[{t:"p",text:"Occasionally a court treats the company and the people behind it as one. This is called **lifting the corporate veil**. It happens far less often than people hope."},{t:"list",items:["Where a statute says so. This is by far the commonest route.","Where the company was used as a device to escape an obligation that already existed.","Where there is fraud."]},{t:"caution",text:"Control is not a ground. Owning every share and making every decision is not a reason to lift the veil; it is what Salomon held to be lawful. 'In the interests of justice' is not a ground either, however often it is argued."},{t:"p",text:"If you want to reach a person standing behind a company, look first for a guarantee they signed or a statute that names them. Those routes work. The veil usually does not."}]},{id:"sec-pe-l-company-roles-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Who acted? Whose loss is it? Who is allowed to complain about it?",h:"Three questions to carry away",body:[{t:"p",text:"Ask these of any company problem. Who acted? Whose loss is it? Who is allowed to complain about it?"},{t:"p",text:"Most of the time the answers are a director, the company's, and the company. The exceptions are where the interesting law lives."},{t:"steps",title:"Ask these three, in this order, of any company problem",items:[{h:"Who acted?",text:"Usually a director. The company acts through people, so name the person and the hat they were wearing at the time."},{h:"Whose loss is it?",text:"Usually the company's. A fall in the value of every member's shares because the company was harmed is the company's loss, not theirs."},{h:"Who is allowed to complain about it?",text:"Usually the company. Where the directors are the wrongdoers, that is what the derivative action and the oppression claim exist to solve."}]}]}],quiz:[{id:"pe-q-cor-1",q:"Aina owns half the shares. Does she own half the company's oven?",options:["Yes, half of every asset","Yes, but only once the company is wound up","No. She owns shares; the company owns the oven","Only if the constitution says so"],answer:2,why:"Shares are a bundle of rights against the company, not slices of its property. The trap is treating a small company as if it were a partnership.",source:"Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd [1897] AC 22"},{id:"pe-q-cor-2",q:"A director who happens to be a qualified accountant is judged by which standard of care?",options:["The ordinary standard for any director","The standard of a professional accountant only","The ordinary standard, raised by the extra skill she actually has","Whatever standard the constitution sets"],answer:2,why:"Section 213(2) sets an objective floor and then adds any extra knowledge or experience the particular director has. Half-remembering the section as purely objective is the usual slip.",source:"Companies Act 2016 s 213(2)"},{id:"pe-q-cor-3",q:"Aina secretly takes a catering job that was offered to the company. Who is the proper claimant?",options:["Rosli, as the other shareholder","Rosli and the company together","The Companies Commission","The company"],answer:3,why:"The duty is owed to the company, so the loss is the company's. Rosli's route is to get permission to sue in the company's name, not to sue in his own.",source:"Companies Act 2016 ss 347-350; the duty is owed to the company"},{id:"pe-q-cor-4",q:"Which of these on its own is a reason to lift the corporate veil?",options:["None of them","One person owns every share","One person makes every decision","The company has almost no money"],answer:0,why:"Control and thin capital are ordinary features of small companies. Lifting the veil needs a statute, fraud, or the use of the company to evade an existing obligation.",source:"Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd [1897] AC 22; control alone is not enough"},{id:"pe-q-cor-5",q:"The company made a profit this year. Can a shareholder simply take a share of it?",options:["Yes, in proportion to shares held","Yes, if the other shareholders agree informally","Yes, but only in cash","No. Profit reaches shareholders only as a declared dividend"],answer:3,why:"Until a dividend is properly declared, the profit is the company's money. Helping yourself to it is a director taking company property.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777); profit reaches members only as a declared dividend"}]},{id:"l-company",moduleId:"m09-company",title:"The company as a person, and the duties that follow",minutes:30,summary:"Separate legal personality, the narrow circumstances in which a court looks behind it, and what the Companies Act 2016 asks of directors.",plants:[],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"companies-act-annotation",where:"the annotations to the directors' duties provisions, read alongside the sections themselves"}],source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), ss 213, 218; Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd [1897] AC 22.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Read ss 213 and 218 in a current reprint of Act 777 and confirm the section numbers for the derivative action and the oppression remedy before citing them — they are not quoted here for that reason. Treat any text predating 2016 as superseded.",plate:"veil",plateCaption:"The veil, and the narrow grounds for looking behind it. Control alone is never one of them.",readMinutes:11,sections:[{id:"sec-l-company-0",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Two siblings, one catering company, and the question of whose debt it is.",h:"Two people and a company",body:[{t:"p",text:"Kavitha and her brother run a catering business together. They register a company called Sedap Catering Sdn Bhd, and each of them takes half of the shares."},{t:"p",text:"The company borrows RM60,000 for a new oven, and eighteen months later the business fails. The bank wants its money back. Who exactly owes it?"},{t:"predict",prompt:"Sedap Catering has failed and the bank wants its RM60,000 back. Before you read on — who owes it?",options:["Kavitha and her brother, half each — it is their business","Kavitha, because she signed the loan papers for the company","The company, and nobody else"],answer:2,reveal:"The company. In every ordinary sense the business is the two of them, and the first answer is how anyone would describe it over the phone. The law says something else, and that one move is the foundation of the whole subject. The third answer is the near miss. Signing for a company is signing as the company, unless you also give a personal guarantee."},{t:"p",text:"The answer is the company, and not Kavitha. That answer is stranger than it first looks, and everything else in company law follows from it."}]},{id:"sec-l-company-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A company is a person in law, and its members are not liable simply for being members.",h:"The company is a person",body:[{t:"p",text:"A company registered under the Act is treated as a person in its own right, separate from the people who own it and run it. It can own property, make contracts, and sue and be sued in its own name."},{t:"p",text:"It also carries on existing as members come and go, which an ordinary partnership does not."},{t:"rule",text:"A company is a body corporate with legal personality separate from its members. Its members are not, by reason only of membership, liable for its obligations.",source:"Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd [1897] AC 22; Companies Act 2016"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: the company owes its own debts, and being a shareholder does not by itself make you liable for them."},{t:"p",text:"Salomon is the case that settled it. The House of Lords upheld the separateness even though one man owned nearly all the shares, ran the business himself, and was also its secured creditor. The company was not his agent and it was not a sham. It was simply a different person."},{t:"p",text:"**Limited liability** is a consequence of that idea rather than the idea itself. Because the company owes the debt, a member risks only whatever is still unpaid on their shares."},{t:"example",text:"Sedap Catering owes the bank RM60,000 and has RM4,000 left in its account. The bank can take the RM4,000. It cannot take Kavitha's car or her personal savings, unless she signed a personal guarantee for the loan."},{t:"compare",title:"Two persons, and only one of them owes the bank",left:{h:"Kavitha",items:["Owns shares in Sedap Catering Sdn Bhd.","Does not own the ovens; the company does.","Risks only what she agreed to pay for her shares.","Her car and her savings are not available to the bank."]},right:{h:"Sedap Catering Sdn Bhd",items:["A person in its own right, able to own property and to be sued.","Owns the ovens and owes the RM60,000.","Its RM4,000 is what the bank can take.","Carries on existing as members come and go."]}}]},{id:"sec-l-company-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Courts look behind that personality rarely, and control alone is never a reason.",h:"Lifting the veil",body:[{t:"p",text:"Sometimes a court will look behind the separate personality and treat the company and the person controlling it as one. Lawyers call this **lifting the veil**, the veil being the separation between a company and its members."},{t:"p",text:"The circumstances in which it happens are much narrower than most people expect. Saying that the veil should be lifted because the result feels unfair is not an argument at all."},{t:"list",items:["Statute — an Act expressly places liability on directors or members. This is by far the commonest route.","Fraud or evasion — the company form is used to escape an obligation that already existed, or to conceal wrongdoing.","Agency — the company is genuinely acting as agent for its controller on ordinary agency principles, rather than merely being controlled by them."]},{t:"caution",text:"Control alone is never enough. A company owned entirely by another company is still a separate person from its parent, and a company with a single member is still separate from that member. That is precisely what Salomon decided, so an answer that lifts the veil because the defendant owned all the shares has not identified a ground."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A man owns every share in his company and makes every decision himself. The company cannot pay a supplier. Can the supplier come after him personally?",options:["No, unless there is a ground to lift the veil, such as statute, fraud or genuine agency","Yes — he owns the whole thing, so the company is really him","Yes, because a company with a single owner is not truly separate"],answer:0,why:"Control is never a ground on its own. That is the precise point Salomon decided, on facts very like these. The first and third answers are the same mistake in two costumes. A ground has to be named, and *this feels unfair* is not one of them."}]},{id:"sec-l-company-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The Companies Act 2016 changed the structure, so a pre-2017 textbook is describing a repealed Act.",h:"What the 2016 Act changed",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Companies Act 2016 replaced the 1965 Act and made structural changes that older textbooks simply do not reflect. Three of them are worth knowing before you read anything else on the subject."},{t:"list",items:["The memorandum and articles of association were replaced by a single, optional **constitution**. A company may have none at all, and then the Act supplies its internal rules.","A private company may now be formed and run with a single member and a single director.","Authorised capital and the par value of shares were abolished, so shares are issued without a nominal value."]},{t:"caution",text:"This is the module where an out-of-date text does the most damage. If a book refers to the memorandum and articles, or to authorised capital, as current law, it predates 2016 and should be treated as history."}]},{id:"sec-l-company-4",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Directors must act for a proper purpose and in good faith, with reasonable care, skill and diligence.",h:"What a director must do",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Act sets out the core duties of a director. They sit alongside the older equitable and common law duties rather than replacing them."},{t:"rule",text:"A director shall at all times exercise his powers in accordance with the Act, for a proper purpose and in good faith in the best interest of the company.",source:"Companies Act 2016, s 213(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: use the powers of the office for the reason they were given, honestly, and in the company's interests rather than your own."},{t:"p",text:"The second duty is about competence rather than loyalty, and its shape is worth looking at closely."},{t:"rule",text:"A director shall exercise reasonable care, skill and diligence with the knowledge, skill and experience which may reasonably be expected of a director having the same responsibilities, and any additional knowledge, skill and experience which the director in fact has.",source:"Companies Act 2016, s 213(2)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: there is a floor set by the job itself, and it rises for a director who happens to know more. An accountant on the board is held to more than the minimum, and a director who takes no interest in the company is not held to less."},{t:"example",text:"Kavitha's brother never reads the accounts and signs whatever is put in front of him. He cannot answer a claim by saying that he did not understand any of it. The standard is what a director in his position ought reasonably to have known."},{t:"p",text:"Two further points recur. The duty is owed to the company, not to individual shareholders, and not in the ordinary case to creditors either."},{t:"p",text:"And a director must not use their position, or the company's property, information or opportunity, to gain a benefit for themselves or to cause harm to the company."},{t:"example",text:"A director hears in a board meeting about a contract the company is chasing, and quietly takes it for himself. That the company might have failed to win it anyway is usually no answer. The objection is to using his position and the information, not to the loss."}]},{id:"sec-l-company-5",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The duty is owed to the company, which is why a single unhappy member usually cannot sue on it.",h:"Who may complain",body:[{t:"p",text:"Because the duty is owed to the company, the company is the proper claimant when the duty is broken. That is where the rule comes from that a single member cannot ordinarily sue for a wrong done to the company."},{t:"p",text:"Two statutory routes exist around that rule. A **derivative action** is a member suing on the company's behalf, with the court's leave. An **oppression** claim is a member's own remedy for being treated unfairly in the conduct of the company's affairs."},{t:"caution",text:"Keep the two apart. A derivative action vindicates the company's right, while an oppression claim vindicates the member's own. Confusing them is a standard way of answering the wrong question."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"Whose right is being vindicated?",caption:"Answer that first and the route follows. Pleading an oppression claim as though it vindicated the company's right is a standard way of losing an otherwise good case.",alt:"A question — whose right was broken? — branching two ways. The company's: a derivative action, brought by a member on the company's behalf, with the court's leave. The member's own: an oppression claim, brought in the member's own name.",question:"Whose right was broken?",branches:[{cond:"the company's",label:"Derivative action",note:"a member sues on its behalf, with leave"},{cond:"the member's own",label:"Oppression claim",note:"brought in the member's own name"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-company-6",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"There is no primary text on the list — the Act with an annotation beside it is the way in.",h:"What to do with this",body:[{t:"p",text:"There are no flashcards or problems for this module yet, and the reading list has no primary text. The Act itself, with an annotation beside it, is the way in."},{t:"p",text:"Read sections 213 and 218 directly. They are short, and most of the duty material in any textbook is commentary on them."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qcm1",q:"What follows from a company having its own legal personality?",options:["The company cannot be sued","Directors are always personally liable","Members are not, by reason only of membership, liable for the company's debts","The company's property belongs to its members"],answer:2,why:"Everything else in the module follows from this one idea.",source:"Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd [1897] AC 22; Companies Act 2016 (Act 777)"},{id:"qcm2",q:"What is the commonest route to making a director personally liable?",options:["Lifting the veil for fraud","A statute that expressly imposes liability","Agency","The fact that he controls the company"],answer:1,why:"Prefer the statutory route where one exists, because the common law grounds are narrow.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777); the statutory provisions imposing personal liability on a director"},{id:"qcm3",q:"Is controlling a company enough to lift the veil?",options:["Yes, where control is total","No — control alone is never enough","Yes, in a one-member company","Yes, as between parent and subsidiary"],answer:1,why:"A wholly owned subsidiary is still a separate person from its parent. That is what Salomon decided.",source:"Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd [1897] AC 22"},{id:"qcm4",q:"What did the Companies Act 2016 replace the memorandum and articles with?",options:["A shareholders' agreement","Model articles that cannot be changed","A single optional constitution","Nothing — they were kept"],answer:2,why:"A company may have no constitution at all, and then the Act supplies its internal rules.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777); the constitution replaced the memorandum and articles"},{id:"qcm5",q:"What is the difference between a derivative action and an oppression claim?",options:["They are two names for the same remedy","A derivative action vindicates the company's right; an oppression claim vindicates the member's own","A derivative action needs no leave","An oppression claim is brought by the company"],answer:1,why:"Confusing them is a standard and very visible error.",source:"Companies Act 2016 ss 347-350 (derivative action) and s 346 (oppression)"}]},{id:"l-shares-and-capital",moduleId:"m09-company",title:"Shares, capital, and what a shareholder actually owns",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Buying shares in a company does not buy you a slice of its lorries or its bank account. It buys a bundle of rights against the company, and the law is strict about when the company may hand money back.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the Parts on share capital, on distributions to shareholders, and on the register and transfer of shares.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for the nature of a share, the abolition of par value, distributions to shareholders, reduction of capital or the register of members on purpose; confirm each in the current Companies Act 2016 as published by the Companies Commission of Malaysia and record the date you checked. Confirm as well the twelve month solvency window for a distribution and the cap on the number of members a private company may have.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"companies-act-2016",where:"the Part on shares, debentures and charges: read the divisions on share capital and on distributions to shareholders, then the provisions on the register of members and on transfers"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-shares-and-capital-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Buying shares buys rights against the company, not a share of the vans in its yard.",h:"Hafiz buys thirty per cent",body:[{t:"p",text:"Hafiz pays RM60,000 for 3,000 shares in Kedai Perabot Mawar Sdn Bhd. That is thirty per cent of the shares on issue. The company owns two delivery vans and a warehouse of furniture."},{t:"p",text:"A year later he falls out with the other two shareholders. He wants one of the vans. He reasons that thirty per cent of the company must be thirty per cent of everything in it."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Hafiz holds thirty per cent of the shares. Can he take one of the company's two vans, or force the company to sell him one?",options:["No, because the vans belong to the company and he owns shares in it","Yes, because he owns thirty per cent of every asset","Only if the other two shareholders agree in writing"],answer:0,reveal:"The vans belong to the company. Hafiz owns shares. Those are two different kinds of property, and holding the second does not carry a slice of the first. If you picked the first answer, you reasoned the way most new shareholders do."},{t:"p",text:"The company is a person in law. It owns the vans. Hafiz owns shares in that person, and a share is a different thing altogether."},{t:"rule",text:"A share is personal property of the member. It carries rights against the company: to vote at meetings, to be paid a dividend when one is properly declared, and to share in any surplus when the company is wound up. It does not give the member any legal or beneficial interest in the property of the company.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the provisions on the nature and transferability of shares, read with the separate legal personality principle in Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd [1897] AC 22."},{t:"p",text:"So ask two questions, in this order. What does the company own? And what does the member hold against the company?"}]},{id:"sec-l-shares-and-capital-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Under the 2016 Act shares have no par value, so the figure on an old certificate values nothing.",h:"Shares with no face value",body:[{t:"p",text:"Older Malaysian share certificates carry a figure. RM1 per share, usually. People read that as what the share is worth."},{t:"p",text:"It never was. Par value was a floor for issuing, not a valuation. The Companies Act 2016 removed the concept."},{t:"rule",text:"Shares issued by a company incorporated under the Companies Act 2016 have no par or nominal value. The whole of what a subscriber pays for a share forms part of the company's share capital. The share premium account, the capital redemption reserve and the ceiling formerly called authorised capital went with par value.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the share capital provisions abolishing par value, together with the transitional provisions for companies incorporated under the repealed Companies Act 1965."},{t:"table",caption:"Share capital before and after the Companies Act 2016",columns:["Old concept","What it is now"],rows:[["Par or nominal value printed on each share","Gone. A share has an issue price and no face value."],["Authorised capital, a ceiling written into the constitution","Gone. The company issues what the members allow it to issue."],["Share premium account","Merged into share capital."],["Capital redemption reserve","Merged into share capital."]],alt:"A table of four old share capital concepts and what became of each. Par value and authorised capital were abolished outright. The share premium account and the capital redemption reserve were both merged into ordinary share capital."},{t:"p",text:"This matters when you read an old document. A constitution drafted in 2010 may still speak of authorised capital. That clause now describes nothing."},{t:"caution",text:"Do not tell a client their shares are worth RM1 each because the certificate says so. Value comes from what the company is worth and from the rights the share carries."}]},{id:"sec-l-shares-and-capital-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A company pays dividends out of profits, and only while its directors are sure it stays solvent.",h:"Taking money out of the company",body:[{t:"p",text:"Hafiz wants a return on his RM60,000. Money moves lawfully from a company to a shareholder in only a few ways."},{t:"list",items:["**A dividend**, declared out of profits available for distribution.","**Salary or fees**, if the shareholder also works for the company and the payment was properly approved.","**Repayment of a loan** the shareholder made to the company.","**A share of the surplus** on a winding up, once every creditor has been paid in full."]},{t:"rule",text:"A company may only make a distribution to its shareholders out of profits available for distribution, and only if the company is solvent. The directors must be satisfied that the company will be able to pay its debts as they become due within twelve months after the distribution. A director who authorises a distribution knowing the company is not solvent may be ordered to repay the amount to the company.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the provisions on distributions to shareholders and on the liability of directors for improper distributions."},{t:"caution",text:"Drawing cash through the year and calling it a dividend at audit time is not a dividend. With no distributable profit behind it, the payment is a return of capital and it can be clawed back."},{t:"p",text:"Reducing share capital is a separate exercise with its own procedure. A company does it either on a solvency statement signed by the directors, or with the confirmation of the court. Neither is a matter of writing a cheque."}]},{id:"sec-l-shares-and-capital-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The register of members decides who is a member; a signed transfer form on its own does not.",h:"Becoming a member, and getting out",body:[{t:"p",text:"Hafiz signs a transfer form selling his shares to his cousin. He assumes the cousin is now a shareholder."},{t:"p",text:"Not yet. The register of members is what makes a person a member. Until the company enters the cousin's name, Hafiz is still on the register and still the member."},{t:"rule",text:"A person becomes a member of a company limited by shares when their name is entered in the register of members. Shares are transferred by a proper instrument of transfer delivered to the company, and the transferee becomes a member when that transfer is registered.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the provisions on the register of members and on the transfer and transmission of shares."},{t:"list",items:["**Transfer** is a sale or a gift. It needs an instrument, and in a private company the directors may hold a power to refuse to register it.","**Transmission** happens by operation of law, on death or on bankruptcy. The personal representative or the trustee steps into the entitlement without a transfer form.","**A private company** restricts the right to transfer its shares, caps how many members it may have, and may not offer its shares to the public."]},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Rosnah dies holding shares in a private company. Her son is her sole beneficiary. What is his position?",options:["The entitlement passes by transmission, and he becomes a member when the company registers him","He must buy the shares back from the company","He is a member as soon as the will is read"],answer:0,why:"Transmission moves the entitlement by operation of law. It still does not put a name on the register, and the register is what decides membership."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-directors-in-and-out",moduleId:"m09-company",title:"Directors: appointment, removal, and being barred from the job",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"Members appoint directors and can usually remove them, though a private company's constitution can make removal harder. The Act also decides who may hold the job at all, and it treats some people as directors who were never appointed.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), sections 213 and 218, together with the Part on directors covering appointment, qualification, resignation, removal and disqualification.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for the appointment, removal, resignation, qualification or disqualification of directors on purpose; confirm each in the current Companies Act 2016 as published by the Companies Commission of Malaysia and record the date you checked. Sections 213 and 218 are cited by number and should still be read in the current text. Confirm as well the minimum age, the minimum number of directors, and how long a court may bar a person from acting.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"companies-act-2016",where:"the Part on directors: the divisions on appointment and qualification, then on vacation of office and removal, and then sections 213 and 218 themselves"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-directors-in-and-out-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Votes remove a director in a private company only if the constitution has not set up something else.",h:"Norhayati wants her brother-in-law off the board",body:[{t:"p",text:"Norhayati holds sixty per cent of Perabot Seri Muda Sdn Bhd. Her brother-in-law Rajan holds forty per cent and sits on the board. He has stopped turning up and stopped answering."},{t:"p",text:"She wants him removed. She has the votes. The question is whether votes alone are enough."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Norhayati holds sixty per cent of a private company. Can she remove a director by calling a meeting and voting him out?",options:["Yes, always — a majority of members can remove any director","Usually, unless the company's own constitution provides otherwise","No, only a court can remove a director"],answer:1,reveal:"Read the constitution first. A private company may remove a director by ordinary resolution unless its constitution says otherwise, and some constitutions say exactly that. A shareholders' agreement can add a further hurdle on top."},{t:"rule",text:"In a private company, the members may remove a director before the end of that director's term by ordinary resolution, unless the constitution provides otherwise. In a public company, the members may remove a director by ordinary resolution despite anything in the constitution or in any agreement, and special notice of the resolution must be given.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the provisions on the removal of directors of private and public companies."},{t:"p",text:"Removal ends the office. It does not end a service contract. If Rajan is also an employee under a written contract, removing him may cost the company damages."},{t:"caution",text:"The two limbs are not the same rule. A public company constitution cannot contract out of removal by the members; a private company constitution can shape it."}]},{id:"sec-l-directors-in-and-out-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A director must be a living adult, not disqualified, and ordinarily resident in Malaysia.",h:"Who is allowed to hold the job",body:[{t:"p",text:"Not everyone may be a director. The Act sets a short list, and a company that ignores it has a hole in its board."},{t:"list",items:["**A natural person.** A company cannot be a director of a Malaysian company.","**At least eighteen years of age**, and of sound mind.","**Not an undischarged bankrupt**, unless leave is given by the court or by the Director General of Insolvency.","**Not disqualified**, whether by a conviction the Act names or by an order of the court.","**Ordinarily resident in Malaysia**, by having a principal place of residence here."]},{t:"rule",text:"A private company must have at least one director and a public company at least two. Every director counted towards that minimum must be a natural person who is at least eighteen years of age, is not disqualified, and ordinarily resides in Malaysia by having a principal place of residence in Malaysia.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the provisions on the qualification of directors and on the minimum number of directors."},{t:"caution",text:"Residence is about where a person actually lives, not about citizenship. A Malaysian who has moved abroad for good may not count towards the minimum, and a foreigner living here may."},{t:"p",text:"The first directors are named in the application to incorporate. After that the members appoint, by ordinary resolution, unless the constitution provides another way."},{t:"p",text:"One extra rule applies to public companies. Each director must be voted on individually, so a single resolution appointing a whole slate is void unless the meeting first agrees to it without dissent."}]},{id:"sec-l-directors-in-and-out-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"You cannot resign your way out if that would leave the company below its minimum number of directors.",h:"Four ways the job ends",body:[{t:"p",text:"Rajan reads the constitution, sees that Norhayati can remove him, and resigns first. Norhayati stays on the board, so the company still has a director. His notice takes effect."},{t:"p",text:"Change one fact. Suppose Rajan were the company's only director. His notice would leave the company with none, and the Act does not allow that."},{t:"rule",text:"A director may resign by giving written notice to the company at its registered office. A resignation is ineffective if it would leave the company without the minimum number of directors required by the Act, and the director remains in office despite having given notice.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the provisions on the resignation of directors and on vacation of office."},{t:"table",caption:"How a directorship ends, and what each way leaves behind",columns:["How it ends","Who brings it about","What survives it"],rows:[["Resignation","The director, by written notice to the registered office","Liability for everything done while in office."],["Removal","The members, by resolution","A possible claim by the director for breach of a service contract."],["Vacation of office","The Act itself, on bankruptcy or on disqualification","Liability, and an offence if the person carries on acting."],["Disqualification","A court, after a conviction or an insolvent liquidation","A bar on acting as a director for the period fixed."]],alt:"A table of four ways a directorship ends. Resignation is the director's own act and leaves past liability intact. Removal is the members' act and may leave the company owing damages under a service contract. Vacation of office happens automatically on bankruptcy or disqualification. A court may also disqualify a person after a conviction or an insolvent liquidation, barring them for a fixed period."},{t:"caution",text:"Acting as a director while disqualified is an offence. Resigning does not wash off what you did while you held the post, and a liquidator can still come after you for it."}]},{id:"sec-l-directors-in-and-out-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Duties follow the job as it is actually done, not the title on a company search.",h:"The director nobody appointed",body:[{t:"p",text:"Encik Faizal is not on the company search. He is not in the register of directors. He is at every meeting, and the two registered directors do what he tells them."},{t:"p",text:"The Act reaches him anyway. It defines a director to include a person occupying the position of director, whatever the person is called. It also reaches a person on whose instructions the directors are accustomed to act."},{t:"p",text:"So Faizal carries a director's duties without ever having been appointed to anything."},{t:"rule",text:"A director of a company shall at all times exercise his powers in accordance with the Act, for a proper purpose and in good faith in the best interest of the company. A director shall exercise reasonable care, skill and diligence.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), section 213."},{t:"rule",text:"A director or officer shall not, without the consent or ratification of a general meeting, use the property of the company, any information acquired by virtue of his position, his position itself, any opportunity of the company, or engage in business in competition with the company, so as to gain directly or indirectly a benefit for himself or any other person, or to cause detriment to the company.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), section 218."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"Faizal was never appointed a director, but the board follows his instructions. He diverts a supply contract to a firm he owns. What is his position?",options:["He may be treated as a director, so the duties in sections 213 and 218 reach him","Nothing follows, because he was never appointed","Only the two registered directors can be liable for it"],answer:0,why:"The Act defines a director by the position occupied and by the instructions the board is accustomed to follow. Appointment is evidence of the job, not the whole of it."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-charges-and-security",moduleId:"m09-company",title:"Charges: what a lender takes when it lends to a company",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"A company borrows by giving security over its assets, and a floating charge lets it go on trading with the very things it has pledged. Lodge the charge late and the debt survives while the security does not.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the Division on the registration of charges created by a company; and the general law on fixed and floating charges as applied in Malaysia.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for the creation, registration, priority or effect of a company charge on purpose; confirm each in the current Companies Act 2016 as published by the Companies Commission of Malaysia and record the date you checked. Confirm in particular which classes of charge must be lodged, and exactly how many days are allowed for lodging one.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"companies-act-2016",where:"the Division on the registration of charges: the classes of charge that must be lodged, the time allowed for lodging, and the effect of failing to lodge in time"}],sections:[{id:"sec-l-charges-and-security-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"A fixed charge pins named assets; a floating charge hovers over a class that keeps changing.",h:"Sivam's lorries and Sivam's stock",body:[{t:"p",text:"Logistik Sinar Sdn Bhd borrows RM800,000. The bank takes security over two named lorries. It also takes security over all present and future stock and book debts."},{t:"p",text:"Encik Sivam runs the company. He needs to know whether he can still sell stock on Monday morning without ringing the bank."},{t:"predict",prompt:"The bank holds security over two named lorries and over all present and future stock. Which of those can the company sell without asking the bank?",options:["Neither, because both are pledged to the bank","Both, until the bank objects in writing","The stock, but not the lorries"],answer:2,reveal:"The stock. Security over a shifting class of assets is a floating charge, and the company trades on as usual until it crystallises. The lorries are named, so that charge is fixed and the company cannot sell them free of it."},{t:"rule",text:"A fixed charge attaches to identified property at once, and the company cannot dispose of that property free of the charge without the consent of the chargee. A floating charge is taken over a class of assets that changes in the ordinary course of business, and the company may deal with those assets in that ordinary course until the charge crystallises.",source:"The general law on fixed and floating charges as applied in Malaysia, read with the provisions of the Companies Act 2016 (Act 777) on charges created by a company."},{t:"p",text:"The label in the debenture does not settle which it is. A court looks at how much freedom the company was actually left with."}]},{id:"sec-l-charges-and-security-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Crystallisation freezes the class, and from that moment the charge behaves like a fixed one.",h:"When a floating charge stops floating",body:[{t:"p",text:"A floating charge is useful precisely because it does not interfere. That ends on a stated event."},{t:"list",items:["The company defaults and the chargee steps in.","A receiver is appointed under the debenture.","The company goes into winding up.","The company stops carrying on business.","An event the debenture itself names as crystallising the charge."]},{t:"example",text:"The bank appoints a receiver on a Tuesday. The stock sitting in the warehouse that morning is caught by the charge. A customer who bought and collected goods on Monday took them free of it."},{t:"caution",text:"Debentures often carry a negative pledge: a promise not to grant a later charge ranking ahead of this one. Whether it binds a later lender turns on what that lender knew."},{t:"p",text:"Crystallisation matters most on the day a company fails. Until then the two kinds of charge feel the same to whoever is running the business."}]},{id:"sec-l-charges-and-security-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"An unlodged charge leaves the lender unsecured, with the loan called in immediately on top.",h:"Lodging the charge, and the cost of being late",body:[{t:"p",text:"A charge created by a company must be lodged with the Registrar of Companies. There is a short window, counted in days from the day the charge is created."},{t:"p",text:"Miss it and the consequence is severe. It falls on the lender, not on the borrower."},{t:"rule",text:"A charge created by a company over property of a class specified in the Act must be lodged with the Registrar within the period the Act allows. Where it is not lodged within that period, the charge is void against the liquidator and against any creditor of the company. The money secured by the charge does not cease to be owed; it becomes immediately payable.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the provisions on the registration of charges created by a company and on the effect of failing to register."},{t:"table",caption:"What lodging a charge on time protects, and what being late costs",columns:["Lodged in time","Not lodged in time"],rows:[["The security stands against the liquidator.","The security is void against the liquidator."],["The lender takes its asset ahead of unsecured creditors.","The lender queues with the unsecured creditors."],["Later lenders are on notice from the public register.","Later lenders find nothing, and may rank ahead."],["The loan runs to its agreed term.","The loan becomes immediately payable."]],alt:"A table comparing a charge lodged in time with one that was not. Lodged in time, the security stands against the liquidator and the lender is paid ahead of unsecured creditors. Later lenders are put on notice, and the loan runs its agreed term. Not lodged in time, the security is void against the liquidator and the lender queues with unsecured creditors. Later lenders find nothing on the register, and the loan falls due at once."},{t:"p",text:"This is also why you search before you lend. A company search at the Companies Commission of Malaysia shows the charges already registered against that company."},{t:"caution",text:"The debt survives an unregistered charge. Clients often hear that a late charge is void and assume the loan is gone. It is not, and it is now due at once."}]},{id:"sec-l-charges-and-security-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"Between two charges over one asset, kind, timing and registration together decide the order.",h:"Who gets paid first",body:[{t:"p",text:"Two lenders can hold charges over the same asset. Three things sort them out."},{t:"list",items:["**Kind.** A fixed charge over an asset generally beats a floating charge over the same asset, even one created earlier.","**Time.** As between charges of the same kind, the earlier one generally ranks first.","**Registration.** A charge not lodged in time is void against the liquidator and other creditors, so it ranks nowhere useful."]},{t:"caution",text:"A receiver appointed under a debenture answers to the chargee who appointed them. The receiver is not there to look after the company, its directors, or its other creditors."},{t:"p",text:"One more rule waits at the end of the story. In a winding up a floating charge is paid after the preferential debts, and employees' claims sit among those."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"A bank holds a floating charge over stock and never lodged it with the Registrar. The company is now wound up. Where does the bank stand?",options:["It takes the stock, because the charge was validly created","It ranks after the preferential debts but ahead of the unsecured creditors","The charge is void against the liquidator, so the bank claims as an unsecured creditor"],answer:2,why:"Creation and registration are different steps. The charge was validly created and is still void against the liquidator, which leaves the bank in the unsecured queue with everyone else."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-winding-up-queue",moduleId:"m09-company",title:"Winding up, and the queue for what is left",minutes:30,readMinutes:6,summary:"When a company is wound up a liquidator gathers what it has and pays it out in a fixed order. Shareholders stand at the back of that queue, and the money usually runs out long before it reaches them.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the Part on winding up: the modes of winding up, the powers of a liquidator, and the priority of payments and preferential debts.",verify:"This lesson states no section number for the modes of winding up, inability to pay debts, the powers of a liquidator, fraudulent trading or the priority of payments on purpose; confirm each in the current Companies Act 2016 as published by the Companies Commission of Malaysia and record the date you checked. Confirm as well the statutory demand threshold, the period allowed for answering it, and the caps on preferential wage claims.",lastVerified:"2026-09-11",reading:[{statuteId:"companies-act-2016",where:"the Part on winding up: the modes of winding up, then the provisions on when a company is unable to pay its debts, and then the priority of payments and preferential debts"}],glossaryDense:"The rule block is the order of payment itself, and the order IS the lesson. Turning a queue into a paragraph loses the one property that matters about it — that it is a sequence, and each place in it is a different answer for a creditor asking where they stand.",sections:[{id:"sec-l-winding-up-queue-0",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Before asking what a creditor will be paid, work out who is standing in front of them.",h:"Chandra sends her last invoice",body:[{t:"p",text:"Puan Chandra supplies packaging to Bina Kuat Sdn Bhd. She is owed RM40,000. The cheques have stopped."},{t:"p",text:"The company owes a bank RM500,000 on a secured loan. Eleven staff are two months unpaid. There is a warehouse of stock and RM90,000 in the bank."},{t:"predict",prompt:"Bina Kuat is wound up. The cash and the stock are all there is. Where does Puan Chandra's RM40,000 stand?",options:["She is behind the secured bank, the costs of the winding up and the employees, and shares what is left with other unsecured creditors","She shares the money equally with the bank and the staff","She is paid first, because the goods she supplied are still in the warehouse"],answer:0,reveal:"An unsecured trade creditor stands near the back. Only the shareholders are behind her. That is the ordinary outcome, and it is why a supplier's credit terms are a legal decision as much as a commercial one."},{t:"p",text:"A winding up asks three questions. Which route is being used? What is there to sell? And who stands ahead of you in the queue?"},{t:"caution",text:"Selling goods that are still in the debtor's warehouse gives you nothing extra by itself. Ownership passed on delivery unless the contract kept title with you."}]},{id:"sec-l-winding-up-queue-1",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Whether the directors can honestly sign a declaration of solvency decides who controls the process.",h:"Three routes to the same end",body:[{t:"p",text:"There are two voluntary routes and one through the court. Which applies turns on whether the company can pay everyone in full."},{t:"table",caption:"The three routes into a winding up",columns:["Route","Who sets it going","When it is used"],rows:[["Members' voluntary winding up","The members, by special resolution","The company can pay its debts in full, and the directors sign a declaration of solvency."],["Creditors' voluntary winding up","The members resolve, but the creditors choose the liquidator","No declaration of solvency can honestly be made."],["Winding up by the court","A petitioner, usually an unpaid creditor","The company is unable to pay its debts, or another ground the Act lists applies."]],alt:"A table of the three routes into a winding up. A members' voluntary winding up is begun by the members by special resolution, and needs a declaration of solvency from the directors. A creditors' voluntary winding up begins with a members' resolution too, but the creditors choose the liquidator. It is used when no declaration of solvency can honestly be made. A winding up by the court begins with a petition, usually from an unpaid creditor, on the ground that the company cannot pay its debts."},{t:"rule",text:"A company is deemed to be unable to pay its debts where a creditor to whom the company owes a sum exceeding the amount fixed by the Act serves a written demand at the registered office of the company, and the company for the period allowed by the Act neglects to pay the sum, or to secure or compound for it to the reasonable satisfaction of the creditor.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the provisions on winding up by the court and on when a company is deemed unable to pay its debts."},{t:"caution",text:"A statutory demand is not a collection tool for a disputed debt. Petition on a debt the company genuinely disputes and the petition can be struck out, with costs against you."},{t:"p",text:"One further ground is worth knowing. A member may petition to wind the company up on the just and equitable ground. That ground answers a breakdown between the people running the company, not an unpaid bill."}]},{id:"sec-l-winding-up-queue-2",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The liquidator displaces the board and can unwind payments the company made before it failed.",h:"What the liquidator actually does",body:[{t:"p",text:"The liquidator takes control of the company's property. The directors' powers stop. The company itself carries on existing until it is dissolved."},{t:"list",items:["Gets in the company's assets and sells them.","Investigates why the company failed, and reports on it.","Disclaims onerous property, such as a lease nobody wants.","Challenges transactions made before the winding up that put one creditor ahead of the rest.","Distributes what is left, in the order the Act sets."]},{t:"p",text:"Paying a friendly supplier in full a month before the winding up is the classic problem. The liquidator can ask the court to undo it."},{t:"caution",text:"A director who kept the company trading on credit with intent to defraud creditors can be ordered to pay the company's debts personally. Separate legal personality does not survive that finding."}]},{id:"sec-l-winding-up-queue-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A fixed chargee takes its asset outside the queue; the members are last and usually get nothing.",h:"The order the money goes out in",body:[{t:"rule",text:"Property subject to a valid fixed charge is applied to the secured creditor to the extent of the security. The remaining assets are applied first to the costs and expenses of the winding up, including the remuneration of the liquidator, then to the preferential debts listed in the Act, then to claims secured by a floating charge, then rateably among the unsecured creditors, and last of all to the members.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), the provisions on the priority of payments in a winding up and on preferential debts."},{t:"table",caption:"The queue in a winding up",columns:["Position","Who is paid","What it covers"],rows:[["Outside the queue","A creditor holding a valid fixed charge","The charged asset, up to the amount owed."],["First","The winding up itself","The liquidator's remuneration, and the costs and expenses of the winding up."],["Second","Preferential debts","Employees' wages, workers' compensation, provident fund contributions and taxes, in the order and up to the limits the Act sets."],["Third","A creditor holding a floating charge","Whatever the charge catches, once the preferential debts are met."],["Fourth","Unsecured creditors","A rateable share of whatever remains."],["Last","The members","Any surplus, according to the rights attached to their shares."]],alt:"A six row table of the winding up queue. A fixed charge holder stands outside it and takes the charged asset. Then come the costs of the winding up, then the preferential debts such as employees' wages and provident fund contributions. Floating charge holders follow, then the unsecured creditors sharing rateably, and last of all the members."},{t:"caution",text:"Preferential debts are capped. A long serving employee may be preferential up to the limit the Act sets, and an ordinary unsecured creditor for everything above it."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"After the bank takes its charged warehouse, RM120,000 is left. The liquidator's costs are RM70,000 and preferential wage claims come to RM60,000. What do the unsecured trade creditors receive?",options:["A rateable share of the RM120,000","Nothing, because the costs and the preferential wages exhaust the fund","A rateable share of RM50,000"],answer:1,why:"The costs of the winding up are paid first, which leaves RM50,000. Preferential wage claims of RM60,000 come next and take all of it. The queue ends before the trade creditors are reached."}]}],quiz:[]},{id:"l-members-remedies",moduleId:"m09-company",title:"Directors' duties, and what a member can do about a breach",minutes:30,summary:"The Companies Act 2016 states the duties in statute. The remedies — oppression, the statutory derivative action, and winding up on the just and equitable ground — belong to different people and answer different complaints.",plants:["ms-co-01","ms-co-02","ms-co-03","ms-co-04"],prepares:[],reading:[{bookId:"companies-act-annotation",where:"the annotations to ss 213, 214, 218, 346 and 347-350"}],source:"Companies Act 2016 ss 213, 214, 218, 346, 347-350, 540; Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd [1897] AC 22.",lastVerified:"2026-09-07",verify:"Check every section number against the current text of Act 777, and check the current Malaysian authority on lifting the corporate veil and on the leave criteria for a statutory derivative action before relying on the summary above.",plate:"minority",plateCaption:"A member outvoted. Whose right was hurt — the company's or the member's own — decides which of the three remedies fits.",readMinutes:8,sections:[{id:"sec-l-members-remedies-0",readMinutes:3,keypoint:"Proper purpose, good faith, best interest of the company — plus reasonable care, skill and diligence.",h:"What a director must do",body:[{t:"p",text:"Three friends set up a company to run a car wash. One of them is the director who signs everything. He quietly buys the soap and the brushes from his brother's firm, at twice the going price."},{t:"p",text:"A director holds power over property that is not his own. The Companies Act 2016 says how that power must be used."},{t:"rule",text:"A director shall at all times exercise his powers in accordance with this Act, for a proper purpose and in good faith in the best interest of the company.",source:"Companies Act 2016, s 213(1)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: use your powers for the purpose they were given, honestly, and for the company rather than for yourself."},{t:"p",text:"That is a fiduciary duty in statutory form. Fiduciary means an obligation of loyalty owed by somebody trusted to act for another."},{t:"p",text:"Loyalty is one thing. Competence is another, and the next subsection deals with it."},{t:"rule",text:"A director shall exercise reasonable care, skill and diligence with the knowledge, skill and experience which may reasonably be expected of a director having the same responsibilities, and any additional knowledge, skill and experience which the director in fact has.",source:"Companies Act 2016, s 213(2)"},{t:"p",text:"Put simply: a director must take the care an ordinary director in his position would take. If he happens to be an accountant or an engineer, more is expected of him, because he knows more."},{t:"p",text:"Section 218 deals separately with the improper use of the company's property, position or information."},{t:"p",text:"Section 214 provides a shelter, called the business judgement rule. A director who makes a business judgement in good faith and for a proper purpose is taken to have met section 213(2)."},{t:"p",text:"That shelter comes with conditions. He must have no material personal interest in the subject matter. He must inform himself on a reasonable basis. And he must reasonably believe the judgement is in the best interest of the company."},{t:"caution",text:"The shelter covers the duty of care in s 213(2). It does not cover the fiduciary duty in s 213(1). A director who helps himself cannot answer with a business judgement."}]},{id:"sec-l-members-remedies-1",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"A loss suffered by the company is the company's to sue on, even when every member feels it.",h:"Whose loss is it?",body:[{t:"p",text:"The car wash loses RM80,000 through the overpriced supplies. The other two friends watch the value of their shares fall. Can they sue the director for that fall?"},{t:"predict",prompt:"The car wash is RM80,000 poorer and the two friends watch their shares lose value. Can they sue the director for what their shares lost?",options:["No — the loss belongs to the company","Yes — it is their money that has gone","Yes, if between them they hold more than half the shares"],answer:0,reveal:"Usually not. The company is a person in law, separate from its members, and the wrong was done to it. The fall in their shares only reflects the company's loss. The first answer is how this feels from the inside, and that is why the rule has to be met head on. They are not left without a remedy: the routes come next."},{t:"p",text:"Usually not, and this is what catches people out. The wrong was done to the company, and a company is a person in law separate from its members. A wrong done to the company is the company's to complain of."},{t:"p",text:"The company acts through its board, or through its members in general meeting. Where the board is the wrongdoer, that is precisely the problem the law has to solve."},{t:"caution",text:"The most common error is to reach for a derivative action or an oppression petition without first asking who suffered the loss. A fall in the value of every member's shares because the company was harmed is reflective loss. It is the company's claim, not the member's."},{t:"diagram",kind:"branch",title:"Ask who suffered the loss, before anything else",caption:"A fall in the value of every member's shares because the company was harmed is reflective loss. Reaching for a route before answering this question is the commonest error in the area.",alt:"A question — who actually suffered the loss? — branching two ways. The company: the claim is the company's to bring, and a fall in every member's shares is merely reflective of it. The member personally: the wrong was done to them rather than to the company, and the claim is their own.",question:"Who actually suffered the loss?",branches:[{cond:"the company",label:"The company's claim",note:"a fall in every member's shares is reflective"},{cond:"the member personally",label:"The member's own claim",note:"a wrong to them, not to the company"}]}]},{id:"sec-l-members-remedies-2",readMinutes:2,keypoint:"Oppression, the statutory derivative action, or winding up — and each answers a different complaint.",h:"The three routes",body:[{t:"p",text:"There are three main routes, and each answers a different complaint. Choosing between them is most of the analysis in a problem question."},{t:"list",items:["**Oppression (s 346).** A member may complain that the affairs of the company are being conducted, or the directors' powers exercised, in a manner oppressive to members or in disregard of their interests. The complaint is about how *he* has been treated. The court's powers on such a petition are wide.","**Statutory derivative action (ss 347 to 350).** A member, a director or the Registrar may bring proceedings **on behalf of the company**, to enforce the company's own right. It requires the **leave of the court** — the court's permission to proceed. The applicant must show good faith, and that the action is prima facie in the company's best interest.","**Winding up on the just and equitable ground.** The court may wind the company up where it is just and equitable to do so. It is used most often in a quasi-partnership, where a small company was really a partnership and the trust between the members has broken down. It is the remedy of last resort, and it kills the company."]},{t:"example",text:"Back to the car wash. If the complaint is that the company was drained of RM80,000, the loss is the company's and the route is a derivative action. If the complaint is that the two friends were shut out of meetings, refused the accounts and cut off from any dividend, that is about their own treatment. The route there is oppression."},{t:"p",text:"An oppression petition and a derivative action are not alternatives to be pleaded together without thought. They proceed on inconsistent premises about whose right is being vindicated."},{t:"table",caption:"An oppression petition and a derivative action are not alternatives to plead together. They rest on inconsistent premises about whose right is being vindicated.",columns:[{label:"The complaint"},{label:"The route"},{label:"Whose right"}],rows:[["The company was drained of RM80,000","Statutory derivative action, ss 347 to 350","The company's — brought on its behalf"],["I was shut out of meetings and refused the accounts","Oppression, s 346","The member's own"],["The relationship this company rested on has broken down","Winding up on the just and equitable ground","The member's own"]]}]},{id:"sec-l-members-remedies-3",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The company is a separate person, and courts look behind that rarely and on stated grounds.",h:"Separate legal personality, and its limits",body:[{t:"p",text:"A company incorporated under the Act is a body corporate with its own legal personality. It can own property, owe money, and sue and be sued in its own name."},{t:"p",text:"Its debts are not its members' debts. That is the foundation of everything above, and of *Salomon v A Salomon & Co Ltd*."},{t:"p",text:"Courts do sometimes look behind the company at the people running it. That is called lifting the corporate veil, and it is done rarely and on stated grounds."},{t:"p",text:"Those grounds are fraud, the use of the corporate form as a device to evade an existing obligation, or a statute that so provides."},{t:"p",text:"Section 540 of the Act is a statutory route of that kind. Where the business has been carried on to defraud creditors, those knowingly party to it may be made personally liable."},{t:"caution",text:"'Lifting the veil in the interests of justice' is not a ground, however often it is asserted. Check the current Malaysian authority before advancing it, and prefer a statutory route where one is available."},{t:"checkpoint",q:"When will a court look behind a company at the people running it?",options:["Whenever it would be unjust not to","On stated grounds — fraud, evading an existing obligation, or where a statute provides","Whenever the company cannot pay its debts"],answer:1,why:"The grounds are narrow and the courts use them rarely. 'In the interests of justice' is asserted constantly and is not a ground. A company that cannot pay is the ordinary case, not a reason to reach its members. Prefer a statutory route, such as section 540, where one is available."}]},{id:"sec-l-members-remedies-4",readMinutes:1,keypoint:"The 2016 Act removed the mandatory AGM for private companies and allows written resolutions.",h:"Meetings, in outline",body:[{t:"p",text:"The Act removed the mandatory annual general meeting for private companies, and provides for written resolutions instead. Public companies must still hold an AGM."},{t:"p",text:"Notice periods, quorum, and the distinction between ordinary and special resolutions are governed by the Act and by the constitution. Quorum is the minimum attendance a meeting needs in order to be valid."},{t:"p",text:"A procedural irregularity may be cured, or it may invalidate the resolution. Which of those happens depends on the provision engaged."},{t:"caution",text:"This is an area where the 2016 Act changed the law substantially from the Companies Act 1965. Treat any source written before 2017 as describing a repealed statute until you have checked it."}]}],quiz:[{id:"qmr1",q:"Which provision states the duty to act in good faith in the best interest of the company?",options:["Section 214","Section 218","Section 346","Section 213(1)"],answer:3,why:"Section 213(1) is the fiduciary duty in statutory form. Section 213(2) is the separate duty of care.",source:"Companies Act 2016 s 213"},{id:"qmr2",q:"Directors have harmed the company, and every member's shares are worth less. What is the difficulty with a member suing personally?",options:["The loss is reflective; the claim belongs to the company","Members may never sue directors","The claim is time-barred","Only the Registrar may sue"],answer:0,why:"A wrong to the company is the company's to complain of. That is why the derivative action machinery exists.",source:"Companies Act 2016 ss 347-350; a wrong to the company is the company's to complain of"},{id:"qmr3",q:"What does a statutory derivative action require?",options:["A special resolution","The consent of the board","Leave of the court, good faith, and that it is prima facie in the company's best interest","A prior oppression petition"],answer:2,why:"Sections 347 to 350 set out the leave requirement and the criteria for granting it.",source:"Companies Act 2016 ss 347-350"},{id:"qmr4",q:"Which remedy answers a complaint about how a member has been treated?",options:["A derivative action","Oppression under s 346","Fraudulent trading under s 540","Rectification of the register"],answer:1,why:"Section 346 is about the conduct of the company's affairs in a manner oppressive to a member.",source:"Companies Act 2016 s 346"},{id:"qmr5",q:"What does the business judgement rule in s 214 do?",options:["Excuses breaches of fiduciary duty","Deems the duty of care met where its conditions are satisfied","Applies only to executive directors","Removes the duty of care for private companies"],answer:1,why:"It is a safe harbour for s 213(2) only. It does not shelter the fiduciary duty in s 213(1).",source:"Companies Act 2016 s 214"},{id:"qmr6",q:"Why should a company law book published in 2015 be treated with care?",options:["The 1965 Act is still in force alongside the 2016 Act","Company law is a State matter","The Companies Act 2016 substantially changed the law","Older reports are unreliable"],answer:2,why:"The 1965 Act was repealed. A pre-2017 text is describing a repealed statute until you have checked it.",source:"Companies Act 2016 (Act 777), which repealed the Companies Act 1965"}]}];export{e as default};
